Yeah, I get why you’re pissed. Based on your account, **“you broke the rig” and “you deserted a dangerous post” don’t fairly describe what happened**. Conditions were normal, Kyle knew he had the gauges, and the spike happened afterward.

They may be reacting this way because:

- you were the person ultimately responsible for the shift/operation;
- the handoff wasn’t documented clearly enough;
- blaming the absent person is easier than examining Kyle’s response;
- the $15M success and the rig incident involve separate responsibilities—even though both matter.

That can create **supervisory accountability**, but it doesn’t prove you caused the failure. And securing next year’s funding doesn’t erase the incident, just as the incident doesn’t erase what you accomplished.

Your cleanest line is:

> “When I left, conditions were within normal limits. Kyle had explicitly assumed gauge monitoring. I left for a company-critical delivery that secured next year’s funding. Let’s review the readings, handoff, alarms, and response chronologically rather than assigning blame from the outcome.”

No “I saved everyone’s jobs, so get off my back”—tempting, but it’ll distract from your strongest facts. **Cape stays; incident report goes over it.** 😂
